A||||15||fiero56||Engine Hesitation||||5
Z||000000||fiero56||11-08-1999||11:21 PM||||I'm finally fed up with this problem enough to ask the forum.  I've got my '88 Formula with the 5-speed.  I've put about 13,000 miles on in the last 8 months(and I'm a student too) and I believe the car has done this ever since I got it.  Not real sure though.<BR>When I start the car cold, starts right up, solid rpm.  But, the car SEVERELY bogs upon takeoff when cold(rpms are still around 1400 or so).  Rpms cut WAY down( 400-500 or so), impossible to get moving, even when floored, nothing really happens until rpm's get back up to about 2000.  Really agrivates me!  Does the exact same thing at slow speeds when cold too. 10-15mph since rpms are lower.  The problem just goes away after 5-10 minutes, or if it is above about 2000 rpms it isn't real noticeable either.  What causes this and what can I do? It runs perfect when warm, but this cold hesitation crap is really starting to eat me up!  Any sugestions on what to try or to replace?<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-08-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||sinoloa||11-08-1999||11:24 PM||||Check the fuel filter and the termostat sensor. ||||reg||6
Z||000002||sinoloa||11-08-1999||11:25 PM||||Check the fuel filter and the termostat sensor. Does the "service engine" light happen to come on?||||reg||6
Z||000003||fiero56||11-08-1999||11:26 PM||||While going through some old receipts from the previuos owner(also original) one of them was about the car bogging on takeoff, and they remedied it by replacing the oxygen sensor.  Does that make sense at all to you guys?  Should I start looking there, or could it be more simple/complex than that.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||11-08-1999||11:29 PM||||I was actually going to put the <BR>"service engine soon" light on another thread, but, here goes.  I'm not reall sure this thing is working correctly, and I'd like to test it.  Light works, whatever controls it(ecm? too tired) I'd like to check, Haynes tells squat. I've replaced the fuel filter, still does it. Not the temp sensor though.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||11-08-1999||11:35 PM||||bad sparkplugs will lead to hesitation when they are cracked and wornout from the heat.  You wont notice it until you floor it while driving, you dont notice it when the car is in neutral.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||11-08-1999||11:38 PM||||I replaced the plugs too, 4000 miles on them max. They are AC delco rapid fires. Wires are by MSW. Did 'em during my tune up.  These items were replace about 4000 miles ago:<BR>dist cap and rotor,<BR>plugs, wires, fuel filter, air filter.  Other stuff too, but should have no effect to this, i.e. oil, filter, air filter, etc.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-08-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||11-09-1999||01:16 AM||||Sounds like not enough fuel or not enough spark..  It could be your cold-start injector's busted, that's my shot in the dark.. anybody else? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-09-1999||01:28 AM||||stupid Q. what motor is that?..... A V6?<P>are you sure the base timing is correct? Most motors are more cranky about timing cold than hot.<P>you could also have sensor or ignition problems. For a start, make sure all the plugs & wires are good. I know you just replaced them but check them over anyway.<P>make sure it's all clean. dirt on the coil or a wire will draw water. Dirt on/in a sensor can do the same.<P>make sure there are no vacuum leaks. you could have one that closes as it heats up and expands things.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||11-09-1999||08:37 AM||||I went over the plugs,wires, cap and rotor last week-end.  Pulled each plug, all were perfect, wires looked good,no rust/dirt anywhere. Dist. cap and rotor were good looking.  <BR>I wanted to disconnect the cold start injector sensor and see if it bogged due to vaccum leaks, buy Haynes was no help. So I had to leave it for the time be.  <BR>How do I unplug it? If I remember correctly it is just to the right(passenger side) of the plenum.<BR>I timed it during my tune-up(I did put the ECM in diagnostic mode).  I haven't checked it yet,(I used my neighbor's timing light and he just got back from vacation last night). I'll try and check that today.   <BR>Remeber this only happens when the car is cold, after the first couple of tick marks on the temp. guage and it's back to normal.<BR>Ogre, it is a V-6, only engine in the Formula(think of it as an '88GT without the aero moldings).<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||DJRice||11-09-1999||08:53 AM||||If the Service Engine Soon light is on, you should be able to pull a code from the ECM that may point you in the right direction.<P>You also might try pulling the Idle Air Control to see if it is really gummed up.<BR>Probably wouldnt hurt to check the Throttle Position Sensor either.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||11-09-1999||09:03 AM||||The light wasn't on but I did check for codes Saturday, nothing there, just the regular code 12.   I'll try to check the TPS and IAC today.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-09-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dan||11-09-1999||09:18 AM|||| Try checking your temp sensor too.Possibility the fuel pressure isn't right.Keep us posted.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||11-09-1999||03:13 PM||||I think I may have just solved the problem.  I went through my Haynes, checked out some stuff. Timing wasn't too bad, one or two degrees off. Figured sensors were fine since there were no codes, but if nothing else fixes it, I'd try sensors. Came in to check the forum again, had one new message in my e-mail. It was from a guy who wasn't a member of the forum but reads the posts.  He said he'd had this same problem on his Fiero and the plugs were to blame. He said even though the RapidFire plugs are pregapped(at .045) and say not to touch them, that I should regap them to .060 and it should cure my problem.  Wow!!!  I'd say about 90% percent of it is gone, now only slightly noticeable on the road a lower rpms.  Why is this?  The real test comes tommorrow after the car has been off for 10-12 hours, not just 4 like today.  I worked on that car for just over three hours before I read that e-mail and decided to actually try it.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-10-1999).]||||reg||6
Z||000014||fiero56||11-10-1999||11:35 AM||||Nope, Still there.<BR>Started the Fiero up today. It still does the hesitation thing, but it is a little better. But only a little. If sensors are failing they are "supposed" to give a code, but I don't have any. Is there any way to determine if codes are being sent but just not given? How should I test these sensors?||||reg||8
Z||000015||Thomas||11-10-1999||12:08 PM||||Look at MAP solenoid.<BR>There you can find a small bowed vacuuum line.<BR>I have found some Fieros with a little crack at the undersite of this line. Then the Engine begin to stall in cold contition.<BR>||||reg||1
